---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description } from '../../content';

const path = '/guides/segmentation';
const title = 'SMS Segmentation on Shopify';
const desc = description(site, 'guides/segmentation');


---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a href="/guides" class="link">← All SMS guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-8">
      <h2>Turn an audience idea into an executable rule</h2>
      <p>This guide is for Shopify campaign operators deciding who should receive a particular text. A useful segment has a purpose, readable logic, an owner and exclusions. “VIP” or “engaged” alone cannot tell another operator why a customer qualifies. Begin with the message promise, then identify the evidence needed to make that promise relevant.</p>
      <h2>Separate eligibility from merchandising</h2>
      <p>First establish whether the person can receive this message on this channel and route. Apply opt-out and operational exclusions before selecting product affinity or spend. Never let a VIP tag override revoked SMS permission. Treat unknown consent, unresolved identity or unsupported destination as a hold rather than converting missing values into permission. Geographic segmentation helps routing but does not determine all applicable law.</p>
      <h2>Document rule semantics</h2>
      <p>Write inclusion groups with explicit AND and OR relationships. A proposed restock audience might require an active request for this variant AND current channel eligibility AND stock available, excluding customers who already bought the requested item. “Bought shoes OR requested sandals AND subscribed” is ambiguous without grouping. Record time windows and timezone interpretation; avoid copying a rule between apps without checking their null values and event semantics.</p>
      <h2>Know which system computes membership</h2>
      <p>Shopify describes customer segments as dynamic rule-based lists whose membership changes with matching criteria. That does not prove a connected SMS app updates at the same time or uses the same fields. Identify the system of record for each field, the synchronization interval or event, and the fallback during an outage. Save a preview for audit, then re-evaluate critical exclusions at actual dispatch.</p>
      <h2>Use product detail without overinterpreting behavior</h2>
      <p>Variant requests and fulfilled purchases answer different questions. Separate gift purchases, returns, cancellations and subscription replenishment before inferring personal product use. A click on a sale link does not establish willingness to buy every discounted product. Build segments around observable actions that support the specific message; use preference capture when inference would be weak.</p>
      <h2>Resolve overlapping segments deliberately</h2>
      <p>A customer can be a recent buyer, a stock-alert requester and a high-spend customer at once. Assign campaign priority and exclusions so multiple audience labels do not create multiple copies of one promotion. Keep an overlap report before a launch. If two campaigns have different offers, decide which promise is appropriate instead of allowing queue timing to decide. Review shared-phone collisions as identity exceptions.</p>
      <h2>Proposed fixtures for rule review</h2>
      <p>Create fixtures for a qualifying subscriber, an opted-out high spender, a missing purchase date, a returned item, a shared phone and a person qualifying for both campaigns. Write expected include, exclude or hold decisions before evaluating the query. Also check a boundary timestamp and a delayed purchase event. These are proposed acceptance cases, not executed tests. Investigate unexpected memberships before adding exceptions that make the rule unreadable.</p>
      <h2>Measure segment usefulness without a false winner</h2>
      <p>Report eligible recipients, submissions, delivery outcomes, orders and opt-outs with consistent denominators. Comparing VIP conversion with dormant customers does not isolate the effect of segmentation because their starting intent differs. For a proposed causal test, randomize within a comparable eligible audience and keep offer and observation window consistent. Small audiences may be better reviewed qualitatively than divided into many noisy experiments.</p>
      <h2>Evaluate exportability and inspectability</h2>
      <p>Ask suppliers to explain one included and one excluded profile, export the rule and show how late events remove queued recipients. Sequenzy advertises segmentation and a paid-plan SMS/MMS beta with US/Canada toll-free scope. Validate the commerce fields and send-time exclusions your store needs rather than assuming a segment label guarantees Shopify parity. Use the app catalog to compare implementation requirements, not unsupported rankings.</p>
      <h2>Sources and related decisions</h2>
      <p>Supplier documentation checked November 25, 2026: <a href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">Sequenzy current product scope</a>. <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/customers/customer-segmentation">Shopify customer segments</a>. Product claims are scoped to these supplier statements; implementation and jurisdiction-specific requirements still need review.</p>
      <p><a href="/guides/list-growth">List Growth</a> · <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">Email Sms Coordination</a> · <a href="/apps">App catalog</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
